### Tested versions

- Reproducible in `v4.4.1.stable.mono.official [49a5bc7b6]`
- Reproducible in `v4.6.1.stable.mono.official [14d19694e]`

### System information

macOS Sequoia 15.6.1

### Issue description

I don't know for sure that this is a bug, but it was unexpected when I encountered it (and it took a _long_ time for me to encounter it in my game 😳). After a `Control` has been `QueueFree`'d, its `_GuiInput` can still trigger. I would expect the engine to check [`IsQueuedForDeletion`](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_object.html#class-object-method-is-queued-for-deletion) rather than requiring my code to do that (for anyone finding this from an internet search, the trivially easy workaround is indeed to check `IsQueuedForDeletion` before acting in `_GuiInput`).

The tricky part is that the `Free` doesn't happen until the end of the current frame, so if your framerate is low (e.g. it's set to 1 FPS in the attached `.zip`) or if you can click really fast, then this is relatively easy to trigger. I think the reason this took so long for me to discover is because I test with a mouse on a 60-FPS device; it wasn't until someone tested on a Legion Go with a touch screen that this happened. I assume it's because it's easy to accidentally produce too many touch events with your finger.

### Steps to reproduce

From a `Control`:

```cs
public override void _GuiInput(InputEvent @event)
{
    if (
        @event is InputEventMouseButton inputEventMouseButton
        && inputEventMouseButton.Pressed
        && inputEventMouseButton.ButtonIndex == MouseButton.Left
    )
    {
        AcceptEvent();
        if (IsQueuedForDeletion())
        {
            GD.Print("Clicked, but already queued for deletion");
        }

        QueueFree();
    }
}
```

